# CHAPTER ONE
## A QUIET KIND OF EMPEROR
### Part One of Three

---

Vekkar Prime did not look like the capital of anything.

From the viewport of the shuttle, Jaina watched the planet slide toward them and tried to square what she was seeing with what the briefing had said. The briefing had used words like *expansionist*, *consolidating*, *atrocities*. The briefing had attached casualty figures. The briefing had described a man who fed governors to their own populations and laughed about it afterward.

The planet on the other side of the transparisteel looked like a back-rim mining world having a slow afternoon.

She tracked the curve of it as the shuttle dropped. Brown continents seamed with red, a single ocean the color of weak caf, three moons spaced unevenly across the dayside sky. The terminator line cut across the northern hemisphere and where it cut, a long band of city lights was already starting to wake. The lights were arranged in grids. Clean ones. Someone had drawn the streets with a ruler. The grids ran along the river systems and along what looked, from this altitude, like a freshly laid speeder rail.

"That's all new," Jagged said quietly, beside her. He didn't look up from the datapad in his lap, but he had been tracking the same lights.

"How new?"

"Eighteen months. The rail. The grid expansion looks about the same. Whatever was here before, it was scattered. Company towns around the smelteries."

She glanced at him. Jag had that look he sometimes got when he was reading something he didn't like and didn't want to say so yet, where the muscle along his jaw set without him noticing. He hadn't shaved this morning. He never did on travel days. He said the stubble made him look less like a politician and more like a man who'd negotiated under fire, which was a thing he had said once as a joke and now treated as policy.

"They built that fast," he said.

"You sound impressed."

"I'm noting it." He turned the datapad face down on his knee. "Impressed is a different word."

Across the cabin, Colonel Niall Voss of Galactic Alliance Intelligence said nothing. He had been saying nothing since they cleared the system's outer marker. Voss was a small, neat man with a small, neat mustache and a habit of folding his arms across his chest as if he were guarding something inside it. He was a career officer who had survived three administrations by understanding which questions not to ask out loud. He glanced at the viewport, glanced at Jaina, and went back to staring at the deck plates.

The shuttle bucked through a layer of high cloud and steadied. Jaina felt the descent in her stomach more than she usually did. She had been flying since she was old enough to reach pedals, and the stomach-drop on a shuttle reentry did not normally find her. Today, it found her. She set her hand flat on her thigh, pressed once, and made the feeling go away.

Across from her, the fourth member of the delegation cleared his throat.

"Permission to speak my mind, Jedi Solo."

"You always have it, Master Bel."

Aren Bel was sixty-two standard years old and looked it. He was one of the last of the survivors of the Praxeum on Yavin, a man whose name almost no one outside the Order recognized because he had spent most of his career as a teacher and the rest of it as a quiet hand on the shoulders of more famous Jedi. Luke had asked him to come on this one. Luke had not said why. Jaina suspected she knew, and had not asked.

"I think," Master Bel said, "that we should not pretend this man is a normal head of state. The Alliance has done that before, with worse outcomes than necessary."

"Acknowledged."

"And I think you, in particular, should not pretend it. He has done things."

"I've read the file."

"Read it twice." Bel's voice was even. He had a careful way of speaking that made everything he said sound like advice you would later wish you had taken. "The first read is to know. The second read is to remember."

She nodded once. She had read the file three times.

The shuttle dropped through the lower cloud layer and the city resolved beneath them in pieces. A central spire, dark grey, blunt at the top. Surrounding it, eight or nine smaller towers laid out in a wide ring, like an honor guard around a king. Beyond the ring, ranks of newer construction, glittering with the bluish work-lights of crews still finishing the upper floors. Beyond *that*, the old town: low, sprawling, the color of dust, threaded with the narrow stamped-permacrete roads of a settlement that had grown around its mines and not around any plan.

Smoke rose from the smelter stacks at the horizon. Honest smoke. White, contained, vented in stages. Whoever was running this place had bought scrubbers.

"They scrub their stacks," Jaina murmured.

Jag glanced at her. "Hm?"

"On Coruscant they don't even pretend. Up here, on a mining world, in an Imperial sector—" she let it trail.

"Yes." Jag nodded. "I noticed."

Voss, against his better judgment, said: "It's propaganda."

"Maybe," Jag said. "It's also scrubbers."

The shuttle banked. Jaina turned her face away from the viewport. She did not want to look at it any longer. The brown haze of any honest industrial world she'd ever seen was missing from this one, and the absence of it made the back of her neck itch in a way she could not articulate.

She closed her eyes and tried to find Mirella in the Force.

The pilot's voice crackled over the cabin speaker. "Beginning final approach. Landing pad designated North Six. Local control has us cleared. ETA, four minutes."

Master Bel's eyes were on her. She felt them before she opened her own.

"Anything?" he asked.

"Nothing clear." Jaina shook her head. "I don't know if she's down there. I don't know if she's alive."

"She's down there."

"You sound sure."

"I'm sure he wants us to see her." Bel folded his weathered hands in his lap. "Whatever he has done to her, he will want us to see it."

Jaina looked at him. Bel looked back, untroubled.

"That's a hopeful read," she said.

"It is the read where she is alive."

She let out a breath. The shuttle banked again, harder this time, and the city tilted up to meet them.

---

Mirella Tarsh had gone missing six standard months ago at the edge of the Hydian Way, where the lane bent past a system called Anaxes Tertius and entered what the cartographers were beginning, reluctantly, to call Maerko Space. She had been part of a joint task force assembled too quickly, briefed too thinly, and led by an Alliance commander who had read the warlord's earlier campaigns as the work of a lucky brigand. They had pushed in along the lane with three corvettes, a light carrier, and a pair of Jedi attached as observers. The lucky brigand had pulled back, drawn them three jumps deep into nothing, and then closed both ends of the trap at the same time.

The Alliance commander had been recovered. Jaina did not like to think about how he had been recovered. He had hung on a public spike for twelve days at the edge of Maerko's own capital plaza, and the holocams had been left running. When the bodies finally came down, the city council on Vekkar Prime had moved with what the after-action report described as *unusual speed* to remove and incinerate them, because the smell had begun, in the report's careful language, *to affect the surrounding commerce*.

The light carrier was now in Maerko's fleet. They had repainted it but not very well. The corvettes were broken up for parts. The other Jedi, a Master Vohrant of Iridonia, was confirmed dead by signal intercept. Mirella was on the missing-persons rolls. Nobody had ever sent a body.

There had been a hololeak six weeks ago. Grainy footage, half a second long, taken from a security camera in some hallway nobody could place. A young woman in worn brown robes, head shaved, a thin metal collar around her throat, carrying a tray. The Order's analysts had not been able to verify it. The analysts had also not been able to *un*verify it.

It had been Mirella's face.

Jaina had watched the half-second of footage forty or fifty times since.

The Alliance had been negotiating around Maerko for almost a year now. Not openly. Through fronts and through the Hapans and through the Imperial Remnant's diplomatic channels, which had been Jag's particular headache, and which had finally produced this: a face-to-face meeting on the warlord's home soil, no entourage, no military escort, no press. Four people on the GA's side. An unknown number on his.

The Senate had argued for weeks about whether to do it. Wynn Dorvan had finally, quietly, ordered it done. He had said, in committee, that the only thing worse than negotiating with a tyrant was negotiating with him after he had taken another sector. He had also said, in private, to Jaina herself, that he was sorry to ask her to go.

She had not asked why he was sorry. She had a guess.

Mirella Tarsh had been her student for two years.

---

The shuttle settled onto landing pad North Six with the soft hiss of a clean set-down. Jaina, who had been a pilot for most of her life, recognized a good touch on the stick when she felt one. Whoever the GA had assigned them had earned his rating.

The cabin lights cycled from blue to white. The ramp hissed unstuck and began its slow descent.

"Final reminder," Jag said, low. "He's the Head of State of these sectors, as far as he's concerned. Address him by whatever title he gives. Don't argue protocol. Don't *correct* him on protocol. If you have to correct him on something, correct him on something that matters."

"Yes, dear," Jaina said dryly.

Jag almost smiled. He did not, quite, but it was a close thing. He stood. He smoothed the front of his jacket once with the flat of his hand, an old Chiss-trained habit she had stopped teasing him about years ago because he never noticed he was doing it.

Master Bel rose more slowly. Voss did not rise at all until Jag had stepped past him, as if he were a piece of furniture being polite about the order of operations.

The ramp came down all the way. Wet warm air rolled into the cabin. It smelled of permacrete dust, distant smoke, and the iron-tang of a world where the soil had a lot of red in it.

Jaina stepped down first.

The landing pad was unremarkable. Reinforced permacrete, freshly scoured, a worn yellow chevron painted at the center. Above it, the sky was the color of overcooked milk, the local sun a fuzzy white smear behind the cloud layer. A breeze moved across the pad and brought with it the high mineral smell of the smelteries.

What was *not* unremarkable was the honor guard.

She had been expecting stormtroopers. She had been expecting them, in some private corner of her head, to look like what stormtroopers always looked like — slightly out-of-date armor, scuffed at the joints, the white turned to bone-yellow by long use, the visible signs of an Imperial Remnant making do with what it had.

These were not those troopers.

The armor was white. Genuinely white, the white of a fresh enamel coat, the white of someone who had a quartermaster who cared. The plates had been redesigned at the shoulders and at the thighs, the joints articulated more cleanly, the trim done in a flat dark grey that did not catch the light. The helmets had the right silhouette and the wrong everything else. The eye-slits were narrower. The mouth grille had been replaced by what she recognized, after a half-beat, as a flat polarized voicecaster panel. The helmets did not look out at her so much as they looked her over.

There were sixteen of them. Eight on either side of a clear walkway leading off the pad. They stood at parade rest. They did not shift. The wind moved the tail of her own coat and did not seem to move them.

Each one carried a carbine she did not recognize. Shorter than an E-11. Bulkier at the receiver. A small data screen mounted on the upper rail. The barrel was thicker than the standard issue and had a perforated cooling sleeve that suggested it cycled fast and hot.

Each one carried, on the back of his armor between the shoulder blades, a curved pack about the size of a folded jacket. A blue indicator light was set into the pack, just below the collar. It was lit on all sixteen.

Jaina, who had grown up around field gear, recognized the shape. Personal shield generators. The kind the old Empire had only ever issued to its inner-system royal guard, and only sparingly, because they were expensive and they ate power and they tended to fail in interesting ways. Sixteen of these, on a landing-pad honor guard, on a *secondary* world, on a *Tuesday*.

Behind her, Voss made a small involuntary sound through his nose. He had counted them too.

She felt Jag at her shoulder. He didn't speak. She didn't need him to.

"Steady," she said, more to herself than to anyone.

The seventeenth trooper stepped forward from the head of the column.

He was — Jaina realized, after a beat — not quite a trooper. The armor was the same shape, but the trim along the chest plate was a darker grey, almost black, and at the right pauldron there was a single short bar of red enamel, the only color on the entire formation. An officer. Or what passed for one. He raised his right hand and pressed two gloved fingers to the side of his helmet. The voicecaster panel modulated.

"Jedi Solo. Lord Fel. Colonel Voss. Master Bel." His voice was clipped and not quite friendly. "Welcome to Vekkar Prime. I am Captain Drask. I'll be escorting you to His Lordship's residence."

*His Lordship.* Jag's left eyelid did not flicker. She admired the discipline of it.

"Captain." Jag inclined his head a precise angle and a half. "Lead on."

Captain Drask turned on his heel.

And as he turned, Jaina caught the edge of something the others had not been carrying.

The eighteenth trooper had been standing further back, between the two ranks, half-hidden by the formation. He was holding a device. It was about the length of his forearm, mounted on a short pole, and at the business end it had a cluster of small open emitters arranged in a half-globe. It was not switched on. She knew this because she could not feel it.

But she could see, by the way her own brain refused to look directly at it, that she would *know* the moment it switched on. It had the shape of a thing meant to make her not exist.

Master Bel had stopped walking. He had not made a noise about stopping. He was simply, suddenly, in place.

"Master Bel?" Captain Drask said, mild. He had not turned. He had not needed to.

"A moment, Captain."

Drask waited. He waited the exact length of time a polite host waits before he becomes a less polite one.

Bel began walking again.

Jaina drew level with him. "Null array."

"Yes."

"Field generator. Big one."

"Yes."

"He brought it onto the landing pad."

"He brought it onto the landing pad, Jedi Solo, because he wanted us to see that he brought it onto the landing pad."

"Polite of him."

Bel's mouth did the small dry thing it did when he was suppressing what Jaina suspected was a much less polite remark.

They passed the eighteenth trooper. The man did not turn his helmet. Jaina was aware of him on a level that was not exactly sight and not exactly anything else. She had felt ysalamiri on a research mission to Myrkr once. This was the same shape of absence and a different texture. The ysalamiri had felt organic, like the Force going around something that simply was not a place. This felt engineered. Like the Force getting *blocked*, not avoided. A wall, where ysalamiri were a hole.

She did not like the difference.

The walkway took them off the pad, through an arched gate of dark grey duracrete, and into a courtyard.

---

The courtyard caught her by surprise.

She had been bracing for an Imperial-style processional hall: black floors, red banners, columns hung with iconography meant to compress the soul of any visitor into the space of about three centimeters. The Empire she had grown up fighting had a very specific architectural language. It was a language designed to be read at a glance, by people whose throats had gone dry on first reading.

This wasn't that.

The courtyard was open. Square, maybe forty meters on a side, paved in pale grey stone that had been laid out in a herringbone pattern by hand. A shallow pool ran along one wall, fed by a small spout cut into the stonework. At the center of the courtyard was a tree.

A tree.

Not a holographic tree. Not an aesthetic flourish. A real, root-bound, planted, alive tree. Some hardy local broadleaf species with dark green leaves and pale veining. Two gardeners in plain grey coveralls were trimming the lower branches. They glanced up when the delegation passed, gave the captain a precise nod that was not quite a salute and not quite a wave, and went back to work.

Jaina counted three civilians in the courtyard. None of them seemed to think this was an unusual day.

A clerk hurried across the far side with a flimsiplast clipboard tucked under one arm. He went into a side door and out of view. A woman in the dark grey-and-red uniform of what Jaina assumed was the local civil service stood by the pool, speaking quietly into a comlink. She did not lower her voice as they passed; she did not raise it, either; she simply finished her conversation, clipped the comlink to her hip, and walked on.

The most disorienting thing in the courtyard was how *un*-disorienting it was.

"Civic ministry annex on the right," Captain Drask said over his shoulder, conversational. "Records office on the left. We're heading through the central hall."

"You handle civic ministry out of His Lordship's residence?" Jag asked.

"His Lordship doesn't have a separate residence, Lord Fel. Wastes space."

Voss's neck went very slightly red at the back, at *Lord Fel*, but he was too well-trained to say a word.

Jaina caught Master Bel's eye. Bel raised his eyebrows a quarter of a centimeter, which for him was extravagant.

They crossed the courtyard. The central hall opened with a soft pneumatic hiss. The interior was, again, not what she would have predicted: pale stone walls, dark wood paneling at hip height, a long carpet running down the middle in a sober deep blue. There were no banners. There were no portraits. There was a single small holosculpture in a niche to the left of the door, displaying a slowly rotating image of the local star system. Civilian work, probably commissioned from a school. The kind of thing a town council put up.

People were *working* in the corridor.

A young man with a stack of flimsi went past them at a brisk walk and threw an absent salute at Captain Drask without slowing. A protocol droid in plain grey plating stood at a counter conferring with a human woman who looked, by her uniform, like a low-rank army quartermaster. Two technicians in coveralls argued politely about a power coupling. The sound of footsteps and quiet voices carried up and down the corridor like the sound in any administrative building anywhere.

This was not the operational center of a warlord, she thought. This was a *town hall.*

"Captain," she said.

"Jedi Solo."

"How many people work in this building?"

"Eighteen hundred and forty-three at last roster count, ma'am. Three shifts. We're on the middle one."

"In the residence."

"In the residence."

She did not look at Jag. Jag did not look at her. Voss, at her left elbow, made another of his small involuntary nose-sounds.

They walked.

She was aware of being watched in a hundred small ways and not in any of the obvious ones. Nobody stared. Nobody slowed. The watching was procedural, woven into the building. A camera here, a sensor there, a security officer in a side niche pretending to consult a datapad. The watching was the watching of an operation that did not need to advertise itself because it was always running.

She tried, gently, to extend her senses.

The corridor closed around her like a hand.

She pulled back at once. *Another null field.* Not as strong as the device on the landing pad, but wider, more diffuse. The walls themselves, or something woven into them. Not enough to cut her off. Enough to let her know the cut-off was *there*, available, at someone's switch.

Master Bel walked half a step closer to her after that, without seeming to.

They turned left, then right, then down a short flight of broad shallow steps. The corridor opened into a smaller antechamber, lined with low padded benches and lit by a soft yellow ceiling panel. A side desk held a tea service. The protocol on it was old Corellian: a wide squat pot, four cups, no saucers. Steam rose from the spout. Someone had made it about three minutes ago.

Captain Drask gestured to the benches.

"His Lordship will receive you shortly. There is tea, if you'd like. The cups are clean."

He turned and stationed himself at the door they had not yet gone through. The eighteenth trooper, the one with the null array, had not followed them into the corridor; he had stayed at the courtyard. Jaina was almost sure of this, and was very deliberately not turning her head to confirm.

Jag did not sit. Voss did not sit. Master Bel sat at once, with the practiced ease of a man who had spent a great deal of his life waiting for important people in waiting rooms, and who saw no need to make a point of it.

Jaina poured herself a cup of tea.

The smell of it surprised her. It was a real blend, faintly smoky, with a peppered note at the back end of the inhale. Not synthesized. Not powdered. *Brewed.* Someone in this building, ten minutes ago, had measured leaves into a pot.

She set the cup down without drinking.

"Master Bel," she said, very quietly.

"Yes."

"I've changed my mind about something."

"Hm."

"I don't think we're going to surprise him."

Bel looked up at her. His eyes, dark and tired and entirely undisturbed, met hers.

"No, Jedi Solo," he said. "I don't think we are."

---

They waited eleven minutes. Jaina counted.

Eleven minutes was not, by the standards of Imperial protocol, a long wait. The old Empire had been known to keep New Republic envoys in antechambers for whole afternoons as a matter of policy. The Hapans had once kept her grandmother waiting for six hours. By that measure, eleven minutes was almost an apology.

She did not let herself be reassured by it. Eleven minutes was the time it took someone to finish what he was already doing.

The door at the inner end of the antechamber slid open with the same soft hiss as every other door in the building, and a young woman in the grey-and-red civil uniform stepped through. She was perhaps twenty-six, plain-faced, hair tied back, with the small efficient movements of someone who had spent the last two years getting very good at not wasting steps.

"Jedi Solo. Lord Fel. Master Bel. Colonel." She nodded once at each, in the right order. Jaina noticed the right order. "His Lordship will see you now. This way, please."

Jag straightened the front of his jacket again. Jaina set the untouched tea down on the desk. Master Bel rose without using his hands.

They followed the aide through the inner door.

The room beyond was — once more — not what she had braced for.

It was an office.

A working office, not a throne room. A long room with a high ceiling, lit by tall windows along one wall that opened onto the smelter horizon. The light came in slanted and dust-bright. The walls were the same pale stone, panelled at hip-height in the same dark wood. There was a long worktable down the center of the room, scattered with flimsiplast and datapads and one half-eaten ration bar. A holoprojector at the far end was running a muted feed of what looked like cargo manifests. There were three chairs. There was, against one wall, a battered repulsor-bench rack with a set of plate weights stacked beside it, the kind of free weights a soldier kept for cell-training when he didn't trust the local gym. A towel was thrown over the bar of the rack.

On a low side-table near the windows, a copper hookah pipe stood unlit. The hose was coiled neatly. The bowl was clean.

In the middle of the room, a man was leaning over the worktable with both hands flat on it, reading something. He looked up as they came in.

This, Jaina realized, was Jiyad Maerko.

He was — and the realization was its own small shock — *ordinary*.

He was not too tall. He was not too short. Somewhere around average for human male, maybe a centimeter under, broad through the chest and shoulders the way men got broad when they lifted heavy weights three times a week for fifteen years. His hair was dark and cropped short, the practical haircut of someone who had a barber's clipper at home and used it on himself in the fresher mirror. His face was — there wasn't a polite word for it — *forgettable*. Squarish jaw. Even features. No scars. A short, neat dark beard along the jaw, kept close.

He looked, Jaina thought with a small unpleasant lurch, like a man you would pass on the loading dock of a freight transfer station and not remember an hour later.

His mouth was set in a small frown. Not an angry frown. The frown of a man who had been told, sometime in his early teens, that his natural resting face looked rude, and who had decided not to do anything about it.

He was wearing dark fatigues. No insignia. The cuffs of the jacket were rolled once. There was a small smear of something — ink, possibly grease — on the back of his left wrist.

"Right," he said. His voice was a baritone with the rounded edges of a Core-Worlds working-class accent, the kind of voice she had heard on a thousand non-commissioned officers across two decades of war. "Princess Solo. Lord Fel. The Jedi. The colonel."

He did not move from the table. He gestured at the three chairs with the back of his hand.

"Sit down. Please. Aide'll get you something to drink if you want. Tea's good. Caf's a war crime, I won't make you drink it."

Nobody sat down.

Maerko looked at them looking at him. He sighed through his nose. It was the sigh of a man who had been through this exact opening five times this month.

"Right," he said again. He came around the worktable, not fast. He walked the way a man walks when his back hurts a little and he doesn't want to advertise it. He stopped at a respectful distance. He extended his right hand.

"Jiyad Maerko. Pleasure."

Jag took the hand. Jag was very good at first contact under hostile conditions and gave away precisely nothing. They shook. Maerko's grip was solid, neither aggressive nor limp, and he let go on the second beat the way someone does who has shaken a lot of hands in his life and learned not to make a thing of it.

Maerko turned to Jaina. He looked at her for a half-second longer than he had looked at Jag. Then he extended his hand.

She took it.

"Princess," he said pleasantly.

"Jedi Knight, actually."

He blinked at her.

He blinked again.

It was, she realized, an entirely honest blink. He was not, in this moment, being clever. He was running her sentence past himself a second time to make sure he had heard it. His head tilted half an inch, the way a man's head tilts when he is genuinely working out whether the person in front of him has said something stupid or whether he himself has missed a step.

She caught the edge of his thought before she meant to. It was loud, the way thoughts were loud in people who had never been around Jedi enough to learn to keep them quiet.

*What kind of title is that.*

Her mouth twitched, against her will. She killed it.

"Jedi Knight," he repeated, slowly, the way a man repeats an unfamiliar food order to a waiter. "All right. Jedi Knight Solo. Pleasure to meet you. Welcome to my planet."

He let go of her hand.

She watched him turn back toward the worktable, watched him gesture again at the chairs, watched him not see at all that anything strange had happened in the last six seconds, and thought, with the small clean clarity that she got when a situation became suddenly more dangerous than the briefing had said it would be:

*Oh. He's actually going to be a problem.*

---

They sat.

The chairs were good chairs. That was another small thing she had not been ready for. They were plain, durasteel-framed, padded in dark blue fabric, with the high straight back of a workman's seat and the firm cushioning of furniture meant to be used for eight hours at a time. Whoever had bought them had cared about the spine of the person sitting in them. They had not cared about impressing anybody.

Maerko did not take a chair across the worktable from them. He took the chair at the head of the table, where he had clearly been sitting all morning, and dragged it back about half a meter so he was angled toward them rather than facing them across the wood. He let himself down into it with the small grunt of a man whose left knee had stopped pretending it was twenty years old.

The aide had not left. She had moved to the side table by the windows and was pouring four cups of tea from a fresh pot. Jaina caught the precise economy of her movements. She had done this for him before. A lot.

"Saska," Maerko said.

"Lord," the aide said, without turning.

"Bring the good biscuits."

"Lord."

"And tell Petriv I want the depot numbers on my desk by sixteen hundred."

"Already on your desk, lord. Top of the left stack."

"Right." Maerko did not look at the left stack to verify. He looked at Jaina. "You see how she runs me. Lucky to have her."

The aide, Saska, did not visibly react. She set the cups out on a tray and began to walk them over.

Maerko leaned back. He laced his hands across his stomach. The hands were broad, scarred along the knuckles in the way of someone who had not always had gloves, and clean.

"All right," he said. "Before we start. Three things. One. You can call me Lord, you can call me Maerko, you can call me Jiyad if we get there, you can call me TK-2208 if you're feeling cute. I won't get prissy about it. Two. There's tea, there's water, there's a Corellian whisky in that cabinet over there that's older than my mother was, and if anybody wants any of it, say so. Three." He paused. "There's a flimsiplast on this table I haven't finished reading. So if I look down at it for thirty seconds while you're talking, it's not a slight. It's a man trying to do two things at once. We clear?"

Jag, who had been watching him with the quiet, attentive expression of a diplomat listening for the trap, said: "Clear."

"Good." Maerko nodded, satisfied. "I hate the dance. You hate the dance. Let's see if we can avoid the dance."

Saska set the tea down between them. Real porcelain. Hand-glazed, by the look of it, with a small uneven foot — a local potter's work, not Imperial issue. She set the small plate of biscuits next to it. She glanced once, almost imperceptibly, at Master Bel, and Master Bel inclined his head a fraction in thanks. Then she stepped back and folded her hands behind her at the edge of the room, where she would be ready if she were called and out of the way if she were not.

Maerko picked up one of the biscuits, snapped it in half, looked at it, looked at Jaina, and said: "Sorry. Hospitality. You first."

He set the biscuit down. He waited.

Jaina took a biscuit. She did not eat it. She set it on the rim of her saucer.

"Mister Maerko."

"*Lord* Maerko, please. I've got the title. May as well use it."

"Lord Maerko."

"There we go."

She watched his face. His face was unreadable in the way faces were unreadable when the man behind them was not bothering to hide anything in particular and there simply was not very much on the surface to read.

"We've come a long way to talk to you."

"You have. Appreciate that."

"We were told you were prepared to discuss terms."

"Was. Am." He nodded. "We'll get there. I want to do the part first where we figure out if we like each other. Saves time if we don't."

Voss, beside her, made the small involuntary nose-sound again. Maerko's eyes flicked to him, mild.

"Colonel. You all right? You keep making that noise. Bad sinus, or are you in physical pain being in this room? I can have a window opened."

"I'm fine, Lord Maerko."

"You sure."

"Yes, Lord Maerko."

"Good. Because if you're not fine we can pause and get you sorted. Whole thing falls apart if my guests are sitting there with their sinuses bleeding into their tea." He said this without any change in tone or pace. It took Jaina a half-beat to catalogue it as humor.

Master Bel, very quietly, made a sound that might have been a laugh and was definitely not one.

Maerko's eyes flicked to him next. "Master, was it?"

"Aren Bel."

"You're the older one."

"I am."

"You're going to be the polite one. The one she sent to remind everyone we're all civilized."

"I am here as an observer, Lord Maerko."

"Sure you are." Maerko's frown deepened a fraction. It was not unfriendly. It was the deepening of a frown belonging to a man who was making a small private adjustment to his mental file. "Bel. I'll remember that. Tea?"

"Thank you. Yes."

Maerko gestured at the cups. Bel took one with both hands. Jaina noted, without meaning to, that Bel had cradled the cup in the old Praxeum gesture, the small inward turning of the wrists that the early Jedi survivors had used as a way of acknowledging a meal in a place where they were not entirely sure of the cook. Whether Maerko saw it, she could not tell.

Maerko picked up his own cup. He sipped. He set it down.

"All right," he said. "Let me say a few things, and then we'll get to your part. I'm telling you these things because there's no point in us sitting here for five hours playing politeness if we can do it in five minutes. You don't have to agree with anything I'm about to say. I'm not asking you to. I'm just laying the cards on the table so you know what colors the deck is."

He paused. He waited to see if anyone wanted to interrupt. Nobody did.

"One." He held up his thumb. "I was a trooper. Imperial Stormtrooper Corps, TK-2208, under Moff Gennadius, garrison three on Vekkar Three when it was still called Vekkar Three. I'm still a trooper. I lift weights on Tuesdays and Saturdays. I smoke a hookah in the evenings. I curse at my staff when they bring me bad numbers and I apologize the next morning if I went too far, which I sometimes do. I'm telling you this because the last GA envoy who walked in here spent ten minutes treating me like I was a Sith Lord in a basement, and I would like to skip that step."

"Noted," Jag said evenly.

"Two." Index finger. "I don't like the Sith. I don't like the Jedi. No offense, Jedi Knight Solo. No offense, Master Bel. I don't like *any* group of organized space wizards making decisions about other people's lives without those people getting a say. That is my long-standing position. It is not personal. We can still do business."

Jaina inclined her head. She did not trust herself to speak.

"Three." Middle finger. He paused as if to register that this was not entirely accidental, then continued. "I'm not interested in the throne of the galaxy. Old man Palpatine tried that, and it cost him everything, including his teeth. I'm interested in *this*." He tapped the table with the side of his hand. "The sectors I have. The ones bordering them. The trade lanes that run through them. The shipping. The mining. The labor. I want this to work. I want it to run. I want the people in it to be — and I am going to use a very small word here, you can decide what you want to do with it — *fed*."

He looked at her.

"That clear?"

"It's clear."

"Good." He picked up his tea. He sipped. He set it down.

"Now," he said, "you talk."

A pause.

Jaina opened her mouth.

The door at the side of the room hissed open.

A man in dark grey-and-red came in fast, a flimsiplast in one hand. He crossed the room without looking at the delegation, leaned in to Maerko's ear, and whispered something Jaina could not hear. It was brief. Four sentences, maybe five. Maerko's expression did not change at first. Then it did. The frown set a little deeper. The corner of his mouth pulled. He nodded once, twice. He held up a finger to the aide — *wait* — and turned back to the table.

"Apologies," he said. He sounded, suddenly, less like a man trying to be polite to envoys and more like a man whose afternoon had just been ruined. "Small thing. I'll deal with it after we're done. Where were we?"

"I was about to talk," Jaina said.

"Right. Yes." He passed a hand briefly across his mouth, dragging the corners of his beard. "Sorry. Go on. Floor's yours."

Jaina settled her hands on her knees.

She felt, behind her, the corridor's flat absence of the Force still pressing against the edges of her. She felt the building around them, busy and humming and full of people who did not know there were Jedi in the room. She felt the small careful watching of the aide Saska at the windows, and the careful unreading of Maerko sitting across from her, and the silent attention of Master Bel beside her, who had not moved his hands since they had sat down.

She drew a breath, slow and centered, the way she had been taught when she was eight years old by a woman named Mara Jade.

"Lord Maerko," she said. "The Galactic Alliance recognizes that you have established de-facto control of seven sectors along the Braxant Run and the eastern terminus of the Hydian Way. We are not here to dispute that fact. We are here to discuss the terms under which that control is —"

The side door hissed open again.

A different aide this time, looking pale.

He crossed the room at a near-run. He did not whisper. He leaned in, said something, half-glanced at the delegation with an expression that was almost an apology, and stepped back.

Maerko sat very still.

Then he sighed. It was an enormous sigh. The sigh of a man being asked, for the third time in a week, to deal with something he had specifically delegated.

"Sorry," he said, to the room. "Sorry. Hold that thought."

He turned to the second aide.

"Tell me again. Slower."

The aide spoke, low. Maerko listened with his head tilted. His face went through several small adjustments. The frown moved. The jaw worked. A muscle in his left temple did a thing that was not quite a tic and not quite anything else.

"Of all the —" Maerko began, and stopped himself. He glanced at his guests. He visibly remembered there were guests. He inhaled through his nose, exhaled through his mouth, and closed his eyes for two full seconds.

"All right," he said quietly. He pinched the bridge of his nose. "All right. I'm going to have to take a minute. Just one. Don't go anywhere."

He stood up. He walked the aide to the corner of the room. He spoke into his ear in a voice that was not quite low enough for human hearing.

It was, however, exactly low enough for the kind of hearing a Jedi could do without trying, and Jaina, who had been trained by her uncle to extend her senses in the precise way that brought audio in without bringing anything else, caught it in pieces.

"— how long ago —"

"— two hours, lord. We just got the second confirmation —"

"— him personally or —"

"— the depot. And the manifest. They have the manifest."

"— oh for — *the manifest?*"

"Yes, lord."

"The new one."

"Yes, lord."

A pause. A long one.

"Right. Put a team together. Quiet team. The good one. Hunt him down. I don't care what rock he's hiding under, I want him by the end of the standard week. And when you find him — make sure the camera's on. I want to see him scream."

"Yes, lord."

"Cut off his —" the voice dropped a fraction further, but not far enough, "— and *feed it to him*. I want him to know what it tastes like before he goes. Recording. The whole thing. Send it to me."

"Yes, lord."

Maerko clapped the aide once on the shoulder. He turned around.

He came back to his chair. He sat down. He picked up his teacup. He took a sip. He set the teacup down. He smiled, briefly, in the way a man smiles when he is composing himself for company.

"Apologies," he said. "Where were we?"

Jaina did not answer for a long second. She kept her face still. She kept her hands still. She did not look at Master Bel. She did not look at Jag. She did not, particularly, look at Maerko.

She looked at her teacup.

Behind her, somewhere on the edge of her awareness, the null field in the walls hummed at a frequency she could not quite hear, and beyond that, very far away, somewhere in a hallway in this building, she could just — *just* — feel a small bright point of life that she had known for two years and had been afraid for, for six months, and that was definitely, definitely awake.

Mirella was somewhere in this building.

Mirella was close.

"Lord Maerko," Jaina said, calmly, picking up her teacup at last, "you were saying you wanted to hear our terms."

"I was."

"Then let's start with the seven sectors."

Maerko nodded once, leaned forward, and gave her his entire attention.

The frown was, very slightly, almost a smile.

